Cold primordial gas at n_H = 464 cm^-3 and 30 K, with cosmic rays at a primary
ionisation rate of 1e-17 s^-1 per H, followed for one free-fall time (2.1 Myr)
at constant density, with redshift 20 for the CMB. No metals, no UV background.
The example compares two networks of geajay:
  network3.yml  primordial_chemistry_network H_He_H2_D (D and HD);
  network4.yml  primordial_chemistry_network H_He_H2_D_ions (adds D-, HD+ and
                HeH+).
Both use the accurate solver and start from the same state: x(HII) = 2e-4,
x(H2) = 5e-7, D/H = 3.4e-5 by number, neutral helium.

The particles sit on a lattice, so every particle has the same state and the
box behaves as one zone. The parameter files take 1024 steps per run: with
fewer steps the temperature in the snapshot lags (about 0.3 percent at 64
steps), while the abundances do not change.

compareNetworks.py reads the last snapshot of each run and prints the H2
fraction, the HD to H ratio and the temperature, with the relative difference
of network 4 against network 3. With cosmic rays the ions change the H2
fraction and the HD to H ratio by more than 20 percent. Without cosmic rays the
change is below 2 percent: run ./run.sh --no-cosmic-rays to see it. The
difference depends on the density, the temperature and the ionisation rate.

Configure SWIFT with the network 4 library:
  ./configure --with-cooling=geajay_4 --with-geajay=$GEAJAY_ROOT

Run with ./run.sh. The comparison needs pygeajay (in the geajay source tree,
src/python) and GEAJAY_LIBRARY_PATH set to the same libgeajay. The two runs take
about two minutes on 4 threads.
